[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 32, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure about a genomic foundation model, labeled with panels **A** and **B**.\n\n**Panel A** shows a workflow for training and applying a nucleotide sequence model called **OmniNA**.\n\nVisible stages:\n- **Stage 1: Pre-training on NCBI nucleotide database**\n  - Input described as an **NCBI nucleotide sequence database**.\n  - Text states a total of **1076.2 billion bases** from **91.7 million nucleotide sequences** spanning many species.\n  - A phylogenetic/tree-like graphic represents broad species diversity.\n  - A **data processing** box shows:\n    - Prefix: “Annotate the following sequence.”\n    - Input: a nucleotide sequence.\n    - Response: described as a Homo sapiens gene sequence related to acetyl cholinesterase.\n  - A **large-scale pre-training** box shows auto-regressive training, tokenized nucleotide text, transformer blocks, and “**1.7 billion parameters**.”\n\n- **Stage 2: Fine-tuning for genomic tasks**\n  - Inputs include genomic sequences such as:\n    - A sequence for promoter classification.\n    - A mutation example with a highlighted nucleotide substitution.\n    - A gRNA and target RNA pairing example.\n  - The pre-trained **OmniNA** model is shown with transformer blocks.\n  - Downstream task outputs include question-answer style interfaces:\n    - “Is the sequence a promoter?” Answer: No.\n    - “Classify the mutation as pathogenic/benign.” Answer: Pathogenic.\n    - “Does the gRNA will off-target?” Answer: Yes.\n\n**Panel B** is a biological/genomic overview illustration.\n\nVisible biological source objects and labels:\n- Chromosomes and chromatin/nucleosome structure.\n- DNA double helix with labeled genomic features.\n- Species phylogeny diagram labeled with domains/groups including **Bacteria**, **Archaea**, and **Eukarya**.\n- Functional genomic elements and molecular features labeled:\n  - **Species differentiation**\n  - **Histone occupation**\n  - **Chromosome accessibility**\n  - **Transcription factor**\n  - **Enhancer**\n  - **Translation initiation site**\n  - **DNA methylation**\n  - **poly(A) signal**\n  - **Splice site**\n  - **Promoter**\n  - **Functional genomic element characterization**\n\nOverall, the figure presents a nucleotide-sequence foundation model pipeline: large-scale pre-training on NCBI nucleotide data, fine-tuning for genomic tasks, and biological applications involving species differentiation and functional genomic element characterization."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 33, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled A-H comparing OmniNA models on nucleotide/token classification tasks and showing biological sequence retrieval/embedding examples.\n\nPanel A: Table of task performance for models OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, OmniNA-66M, and OmniNA-1.7B/330M/66M variants. Tasks include nucleotide sequence classification, taxonomy classification, and enzyme classification. Metrics shown are Precision, Recall, and F1, with colored heatmap-style cells.\n\nPanel B: Example nucleotide/protein annotation interface. Red input nucleotide sequence is shown at the top, followed by “Response from” OmniNA models. Outputs include organism/protein-like labels such as “homogenens voucher,” “gyanase,” “bottle,” “Bacillus voucher,” “Moll,” “Apperson,” and “uncharacterized protein,” with highlighted predicted spans/tokens.\n\nPanel C: Taxonomy classification results. Left: bar chart of simulated annealing or similarity-like scores for OmniNA-1.7B, OmniNA-330M, and OmniNA-66M, with token numbers below. Right: line plot of token number versus token frequency bins. OmniNA-1.7B appears highest.\n\nPanel D: Gene function classification results. Left: bar chart for the same models with token numbers below. Right: token frequency distribution line plot. OmniNA-1.7B appears highest, then 330M, then 66M.\n\nPanel E: Boxplot of cosine similarity comparing OmniNA model embeddings against random sequence. OmniNA-1.7B has the highest similarity, 330M and 66M lower but still high, random sequence much lower. Statistical significance markers are shown.\n\nPanel F: t-SNE embedding plot of biological sequence groups, with labeled clusters including mRNA of dehydrogenase, mRNA of oxidase, chloroplast CDS/mRNA, and subunit of NSL complex. At right is a red input nucleotide sequence and “Response from different repeats of OmniNA-1.7B,” listing multiple similar functional annotations such as regulatory NSL complex subunits and KAT8 regulatory complex.\n\nPanel G: Boxplot of cosine similarity for HIV-related sequences/models versus random sequence. OmniNA models show high similarity, random sequence much lower, with significance markers.\n\nPanel H: t-SNE plot of HIV gene categories with legend labels including gag, gag-pol, pol, vif, vpr, vpu, env, nef. At right, a schematic HIV genome map shows gene segments such as gag, pol, vif, vpr, vpu, env. Below are example retrieval responses from OmniNA-1.7B to a red input sequence, including HIV-1 isolate/patient and envelope-like sequence descriptions.\n\nBiological source objects visible: nucleotide sequences, mRNA/CDS annotations, chloroplast sequences, NSL/KAT8 regulatory complex-related sequences, and HIV-1 gene/protein regions. Transformations shown include sequence tokenization/frequency analysis, model classification, cosine similarity comparisons, t-SNE dimensionality reduction of embeddings, and generated/retrieved text responses from OmniNA model interfaces."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 34, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological/computational figure showing token embeddings, feature representation, conservation, taxonomy, and viral protein analyses, mostly visualized with t-SNE/fit-SNE scatter plots and heatmaps.\n\nVisible panels:\n\n- **A, Token embedding:** t-SNE scatter of token embeddings with clustered points colored by apparent groups. Callouts label example tokens associated with gene names, gene functions, and coding sequences. Examples include ORF1a/ORF1b-related labels, mannosyl-transferase-related function text, and nucleotide strings such as `AA...`, `TT...`.\n- **B, Token example / taxonomy:** t-SNE scatter where points are colored by biological domains/groups including bacteria, virus, and fungi. Right-side callouts list example taxa/groups such as Riboviria, Orthornavirae, Colitvirus, Fijivirus, Duplodnaviria, Caudoviricetes, and Monodnaviria.\n- **C, Feature representation:** Heatmap comparing feature representations across layers for **OmniHelix-1.7B**, with rows labeled by model layers and columns corresponding to downstream biological tasks or datasets. Color scale appears to indicate ARI or best ARI values.\n- **D, Sequence conservation:** t-SNE plot labeled “Sequence conservation,” showing orange and blue trajectories/contours corresponding to high vs low conservation, from a specific model layer.\n- **E, Taxonomy classification:** t-SNE plot labeled “Taxonomy classification,” with large separated regions colored for bacteria, virus, and fungi.\n- **F, Bacteria and archaea classification:** t-SNE plot with multiple colored clusters, labeled as a layer-level representation.\n- **G, Taxonomic tree:** Circular phylogenetic/taxonomic tree with colored clades and labels including groups such as Bacillota, Campylobacterota, Pseudomonadota, Cyanobacteria, and other bacterial/archaeal taxa.\n- **H, SARS-CoV-2 phylogeny:** t-SNE-like embedding with branching/trajectory structure for SARS-CoV-2 variants. Variant labels include Alpha, Delta, Omicron/BA-type branches, and Wuhan-Hu-1/2019 reference region.\n- **I, Pairwise cosine similarity:** Bar or point plot comparing SARS-CoV-2 variant clades, with significance markers. Legend includes Clade 19, Clade 20, Clade 21, Clade 22, and Clade 23.\n- **J, Viral functional proteins:** t-SNE scatter labeled “Viral functional proteins,” with clusters colored by coding sequence categories such as coat protein, viral capsid, matrix protein, viral RNA polymerase, nucleocapsid protein, virulence factor, envelope protein, hemagglutinin, neuraminidase, and spike protein.\n\nOverall, the figure appears to present embeddings from a biological sequence model, showing that learned representations separate tokens, taxa, conservation patterns, SARS-CoV-2 variants, and viral protein functional categories."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 35, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical/model benchmarking figure labeled A-J.\n\nVisible content:\n- **A**: Radar/spider chart comparing models across regulatory genomics tasks: Promoter, Active enhancer, Active promoter, Silencer, Insulator, Enhancer. Models listed include OmniHelix-1.7B, OmniHelix-330M, OmniHelix-66M, and Benchmark.\n- **B**: Bar chart of **F1** for PAS and TIS tasks, comparing the same model set.\n- **C**: Bar chart of **F1** for splice site prediction.\n- **D**: Bar chart of **F1** for gRNA off-target prediction.\n- **E**: Dot/heatmap-style plot for many biological cell/tissue categories, with color indicating **Recall** and dot size indicating **Precision**. Labels include tissue/cell types such as Thyroid, Smooth-Musc, Small-Int, Skeletal-Musc, Prostate, Pancreas, Lung, Liver, Kidney, Heart, Colon, Blood, Adipocyte, and others.\n- **F**: Heatmap table comparing task groups and metrics across benchmarks and OmniHelix model sizes. Task groups include **Chromatin accessibility**, **Histone**, and **Transcription factor**, with metrics such as F1, Precision, and Recall.\n- **G**: Violin plots showing **Pathogenicity of mutation**, comparing distributions across benchmark/model conditions, with significance markers above.\n- **H**: Radar/spider chart over gene labels including ABCA4, USH2A, MLH1, BRCA1, MTOR, MYO7A, RYR1, TSC2, MYH7, SCN4A, ABCD1, MSH2, COX15, XCNQ2, WT1, F8, and F7. Legend indicates EVF and OmniHelix-1.7B.\n- **Lower H**: Bar chart for **Pathogenic virus** F1 comparing OmniHelix models and benchmark.\n- **I**: Bar charts of **F1** at taxonomy levels Family, Class, Order, and Phylum, comparing OmniHelix models and benchmark.\n- **J**: Scatter plot comparing **OmniHelix F1 score** against **Benchmark**, with a diagonal reference line.\n\nBiological source objects shown or implied:\n- Regulatory DNA elements, splice sites, gRNA off-target sequences, chromatin accessibility profiles, histone marks, transcription factor signals, tissue/cell-type annotations, pathogenic mutations, disease-associated genes, pathogenic viruses, and taxonomic categories.\n\nModel interfaces/outputs:\n- The figure presents model benchmarking outputs using F1, precision, recall, pathogenicity scores, and classification performance across genomic and biological prediction tasks.\n\nMain visible finding:\n- OmniHelix model variants, especially **OmniHelix-1.7B**, generally appear to outperform or match benchmark baselines across many displayed genomics and biomedical prediction tasks."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 36, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure about mutation effect prediction and biological protein/domain interpretation.\n\nVisible panels:\n\n- **A**: Schematic of an **omni-helix mutation effect score**. It shows DNA sequence context around a mutation, labeled genomic coordinates near **chr17:41218413 A>G**, and a formula comparing predicted mutation effects using likelihood/log-probability terms.\n- **B**: Two bar/line plots labeled **TIS** and **PAS**, showing proportions of **benign** vs **pathogenic** variants across mutation effect score bins. The green plot is for TIS; the red plot is for PAS.\n- **C**: Bar plot comparing **Predicted** vs **Real** mutation effects across positions/bins, with blue bars and a red curve. The x-axis is real mutation effect.\n- **D-F**: BRCA1-focused protein/domain analysis.\n  - **D/E**: Heatmap-like mutation effect map across the **BRCA1 amino acid sequence**, with labeled domains including **Zinc finger**, **BRCT1**, and **BRCT2**. Red/blue mutation effect scale shown.\n  - **F**: Protein structure visualization of **BRCA1**, colored by mutation effect, with labeled **Zinc finger**, **BRCT1**, and **BRCT2** regions.\n- **G-I**: CHD7-focused protein/domain analysis.\n  - **G/H**: Mutation effect map across the **CHD7 amino acid sequence**, with labeled domains including **Chromo 1-2**, **ATP-binding**, **Helicase C-terminal**, **SANT**, and **BRK** domains.\n  - **I**: Protein structure visualization of **CHD7**, colored by mutation effect, with labels for **Chromo 1-2** and **ATP-binding** regions.\n\nBiological source objects include DNA sequence variants, amino acid sequences, pathogenic/benign mutation annotations, and protein structures for **BRCA1** and **CHD7**.\n\nThe figure appears to present a model-derived mutation effect score, compare predicted effects to observed pathogenicity, and map predicted mutation impacts onto protein domains and 3D structures."]}]

